  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1141 AM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nCurrent Water Vapor and upper air analysis shows ridge over the\nfar western CONUS, while broad upper trough sits over the plains\ninto the Great Lakes region, with northwest flow over the FA. 17Z\nsurface analysis shows a SW-NE oriented surface trough over\nwestern SD early this afternoon. Winds are currently light out of\nthe north on the back side of the trough, while out ahead of it\nwinds are light to breezy out of the south-southwest. Temperatures\ncurrently sit in the 80s to low 90s. Skies are a bit hazy from\nwildfires out west, and hi-rez models keep smoke in the region at\nleast through Friday.\n\nAs the trough moves southeast towards more available moisture, a\nfew CAMS show potential for isolated storm development near south\ncentral SD late this afternoon into early evening. CAPE values in\nthe 700-1400 j/Kg range, coupled with 30-40kt 0-6km shear, could\npromote an isolated strong to severe storm if there is any\ninitiation this afternoon. Main concern will be available moisture\ndown there. Models do hint at a LLJ setup both overnight tonight\nand Friday night. For tonight, CAMS don't point towards any\ndevelopment tonight, given the dry air. Early Saturday morning may\nbe more concerning, with better moisture advection, models do\npoint towards a higher potential for overnight storms with a a\nchance for severe. Currently models show the best location for\nstorms early Saturday morning around south central SD. \n\nUpper ridge flattens out a bit over the weekend, prompting\nslightly cooler temperatures Monday through the remainder of next\nweek, though still in the 80s. With a more zonal upper flow\nsetting up, next week looks a bit more active with shower/storm\npotential. Especially the latter half of next week, as models\npoint towards better moisture advection into the area.",
